 Well, that's what they wanted.

237
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,290
 Why not? Only as things went on, it got too crowded.

238
00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:32,860
 So it was decided to stack all the deer departed in this floating cemetery.

239
00:19:34,130 --> 00:19:38,240
 Space centers for rest and leisure activities, mainly for space station personnel.

240
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:39,930
 What's the big disk on top?

241
00:19:40,580 --> 00:19:42,480
 Ah, that's the captor for solar energy.

242
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:45,000
 Everything comes from the sun, you know.

243
00:19:45,230 --> 00:19:48,900
 It has life, all energy, petroleum oil and wood, all the sun.

244
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:51,060
 Do you know how much energy it sends us continuously?

245
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:54,040
 It's a colossal figure, 250 trillion horsepower.

246
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:55,180
 That's how much.

247
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:56,680
 And what did we do with it?

248
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:58,000
 I'll tell you what we did with it.

249
00:19:58,040 --> 00:19:59,320
 We took sunbaths, that's what.

250
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:00,860
 Hey, there, what's that?

251
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:01,660
 Is that what I think it is?

252
00:20:02,450 --> 00:20:04,070
 Ah, I think you know what that is.

253
00:20:04,110 --> 00:20:05,830
 That is the graveyard of Rex and Hawks.

254
00:20:06,220 --> 00:20:07,460
 If you wanted to see it, there it is.

255
00:20:07,910 --> 00:20:10,470
 As early as the year 2000, there were 400 objects in the sky,

256
00:20:10,550 --> 00:20:12,110
 and I'd hate to tell you how many were there later.

257
00:20:12,460 --> 00:20:15,550
 As usual, it took a tragedy for anything to be done about it.

258
00:20:16,570 --> 00:20:18,750
 A man needs some catastrophe to make decisions.

259
00:20:18,830 --> 00:20:21,010
 You could say he sees his future with hindsight.

260
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:23,140
 What was I saying about all this junk?

261
00:20:23,140 --> 00:20:24,080
 Oh, that's right.

262
00:20:24,420 --> 00:20:25,320
 Well, a lot of junk.

263
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:29,140
 One day, a ship was returning to Earth on automatic guidance,

264
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:31,740
 And in those days, the crew still had to go into suspended animation.

265
00:20:31,740 --> 00:20:35,500
 Well, it collided with one of these wrecks that somehow got in the way.

266
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:37,240
 The shock was devastating.

267
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:41,620
 So it was decided to clean up space.

268
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:43,320
 Put all these objects here in one place.

269
00:20:44,870 --> 00:20:51,200
 Gentlemen, we are now passing over the remnants of the spaceship Invincible,

270
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:54,060
 the heroic craft that defended us in the Great War.

271
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:07,230
 Favorite site for tourists.

272
00:21:07,330 --> 00:21:08,970
 They come here on pilgrimage.

273
00:21:09,270 --> 00:21:09,890
 It's a museum.

274
00:21:10,440 --> 00:21:11,860
 There's leisure center there.

275
00:21:12,290 --> 00:21:15,840
 Then you have the amusement park, full of all sorts of extraordinary games,

276
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:17,400
 fast food pills shows.

277
00:21:18,250 --> 00:21:19,030
 Well, do we go on?

278
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:20,380
 Mm-hmm.

279
00:21:20,520 --> 00:21:21,880
 To the ring of solar mirrors.

280
00:21:32,260 --> 00:21:37,910
 Say again, my young friends,

281
00:21:38,010 --> 00:21:41,390
 the sun isn't only the source and reserve of all energy on Earth.

282
00:21:41,850 --> 00:21:44,700
 It is the very source of life itself.

283
00:21:45,860 --> 00:21:48,540
 Everything you see here functions because of the sun.

284
00:21:50,860 --> 00:21:51,620
 Are we on course?

285
00:21:51,910 --> 00:21:52,590
 There's the ring.

286
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,180
 That's right, to the ring of the mirrors.

287
00:22:03,040 --> 00:22:03,500
 G-ring.

288
00:22:03,980 --> 00:22:04,740
 There, the sun.

289
00:22:05,110 --> 00:22:07,680
 Have you ever considered what the results would be

290
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:09,500
 if the sun's rays striking the Earth

291
00:22:09,500 --> 00:22:11,160
 were not just scattered about,

292
00:22:11,630 --> 00:22:13,310
 focused in a single point?

293
00:22:13,310 --> 00:22:18,780
 All right, it's top secret, only I think you ought to know if the Earth is ever menaced again.

294
00:22:19,180 --> 00:22:24,650
 As it was when you saved us, we now have a way of warding off any aggressor, only it's not quite enough.

295
00:22:29,410 --> 00:22:35,090
 Once it's perfected, nothing will be able to resist it.

296
00:22:35,330 --> 00:22:38,690
 The hardest metals will melt like butter and glow white hot.

297
00:22:42,470 --> 00:22:47,650
 And whole planets will melt, be destroyed, reduced to spatial dust.

298
00:22:48,370 --> 00:22:51,290
 Such a weapon will defend the Earth from orbiting stations.

299
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:53,350
 The Earth will be invulnerable.

300
00:22:53,950 --> 00:22:57,020
 And you're not afraid of creating such a powerful weapon?

301
00:22:57,020 --> 00:22:59,800
 But I told you its use is entirely defensive.

302
00:23:01,070 --> 00:23:02,690
 You don't think for one minute.

303
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:08,320
 That's what they always say at first.

304
00:23:09,330 --> 00:23:10,690
 Of course, there's a danger.

305
00:23:11,490 --> 00:23:13,610
 Anyway, our tests haven't been successful yet.

306
00:23:13,990 --> 00:23:15,330
 Something always goes wrong.

307
00:23:15,510 --> 00:23:17,490
 Some say they think it's sabotage.

308
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:19,740
 Who can say who would do it?

309
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:22,000
 That Cassiopean bunch, who else?

310
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:26,540
 Consider their position if you had had your sun gun ready when their rocket was on the way.

311
00:23:27,020 --> 00:23:28,400
 Are there any Cassiopeans here?

312
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:33,120
 A few working as space technicians, only they're all trusted above suspicion.

313
00:23:33,140 --> 00:23:34,340
 and it can't be any of them.

314
00:23:34,770 --> 00:23:35,760
 Can you be so sure?

315
00:23:36,030 --> 00:23:38,080
 We've had a lot of experience, I can tell you,

316
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:39,980
 and I wouldn't trust any Cassiopean.

317
00:23:40,140 --> 00:23:42,620
 As everywhere, there are good and bad, but Grouch is mad.

318
00:23:42,890 --> 00:23:44,490
 What's that group of people up there?

319
00:23:45,090 --> 00:23:45,810
 Something's happened.

320
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:47,530
 You're right. Let's have a look.

321
00:23:50,040 --> 00:23:54,300
 Looks like he's dead.

322
00:23:56,290 --> 00:23:57,950
 Police, go on about your business.

323
00:23:58,090 --> 00:23:59,150
 There's nothing to see here.

324
00:23:59,350 --> 00:24:00,530
 Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

325
00:24:00,550 --> 00:24:01,770
 I didn't recognize you with the suit.

326
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:05,500
 As you see, one of our officers was killed.

327
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,250
 Looks like he was thrown from his vehicle, sir.

328
00:24:08,250 --> 00:24:09,270
 Was he alone?

329
00:24:10,230 --> 00:24:12,290
 Yes, on routine patrol in this section.

330
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:13,530
 Have you found his vehicle?

331
00:24:13,960 --> 00:24:17,150
 Yes, Lieutenant. It's orbiting around. Should come back in ten minutes.

332
00:24:18,620 --> 00:24:19,900
 It's programmed, then.

333
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:22,060
 Quick, there's no time to lose.

334
00:24:24,370 --> 00:24:27,440
 You'd better stay in the shuttle.

335
00:24:28,070 --> 00:24:28,720
 Yeah, it's okay.

336
00:24:33,700 --> 00:24:35,690
 We'll each take different directions.

337
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:37,110
 He can't be very far.

338
00:24:37,290 --> 00:24:39,010
 Closer than you think.

339
00:24:39,420 --> 00:24:41,200
 You see what curiosity got you?

340
00:24:41,460 --> 00:24:45,300
 Didn't anyone ever teach you it's not polite to poke your nose in other people's business?

341
00:24:45,680 --> 00:24:48,360
 Education isn't what it was in my day. You need a lesson.

342
00:24:48,690 --> 00:24:52,100
 Yeah, okay, you win. Only first, I'd like to know just what's your game.

343
00:24:52,220 --> 00:24:56,130
 Well, I guess I can tell you, since the two of you are gonna die,

344
00:24:56,330 --> 00:25:01,090
 That cop surprised me as I was finishing a little job of sabotage, routine stuff.

345
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:03,560
 He tried to get me to go into his vehicle.

346
00:25:04,770 --> 00:25:07,330
 Oh, he had a little scuffle and he lost his helmet.

347
00:25:07,570 --> 00:25:08,370
 Guess he was hot.

348
00:25:08,970 --> 00:25:11,050
 Anyway, the poor fellow didn't pull through.

349
00:25:11,410 --> 00:25:14,050
 I rode his police vehicle once around and got off here.

350
00:25:14,290 --> 00:25:15,470
 Now, does that clear it up?

351
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:17,240
 Bye, you guys.

352
00:25:17,930 --> 00:25:19,470
 Hey, wait a minute.

353
00:25:19,670 --> 00:25:21,370
 Aren't you those jerks from Omega?

354
00:25:21,590 --> 00:25:22,570
 The big heroes?

355
00:25:23,160 --> 00:25:24,380
 Yeah, sure.

356
00:25:24,950 --> 00:25:26,610
 Now the boss is gonna be happy.

357
00:25:26,610 --> 00:25:29,650
 Okay, you guys, your hours come.

358
00:25:30,870 --> 00:25:34,070
 Now you know what happens to heroes.

359
00:25:37,310 --> 00:25:40,340
 Watch out behind you!

360
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:43,000
 That's the oldest one in a book.

361
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:44,880
 I haven't fallen for that since I was a kid.

362
00:25:46,780 --> 00:26:07,330
 Such a wonder.

363
00:26:13,880 --> 00:26:15,500
 Come take your place.

